<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
<title>Section 1.2 Notes</title>

<p><alert>
    Equivalent systems
</alert> are two linear systems with the same solution set. </p>

<p>Our strategy for solving a linear system will be to replace one system with an equivalent system that is easier to solve. </p>

<example xml:id="ex-linear-system-graphical">
    <title>A Linear System in Two Variables</title>

    <statement>
        <p>
            Consider the system
        </p>

        <md>
            \begin{aligned}
            x_1-2x_2 \amp = -1\\
            -x_1+3x_2 \amp = 3.
            \end{aligned}
        </md>

        <p>
            Solving the system gives
        </p>

        <me>
            \begin{aligned}
            x_1-2x_2 \amp = -1\\
            x_2 \amp = 2,
            \end{aligned}
        </me>

        <p>
            and therefore
        </p>

        <me>
            x_1=3,
            \qquad
            x_2=2.
        </me>

        <figure xml:id="fig-successive-elimination">
            <caption>
                A graphical interpretation of solving a linear system by
                elimination. The first panel shows two intersecting lines.
                The second panel represents an equivalent system after
                elimination. The third panel represents the unique
                solution point.
            </caption>

            <description>
                Three coordinate-plane diagrams arranged from left to
                right. The first shows two nonparallel lines intersecting.
                The second shows a slanted line intersecting a horizontal
                line whose height corresponds to x-sub-2 equals two.
                The third shows a vertical line and a horizontal line
                intersecting at the point with coordinates
                (3,2), representing the solution x-sub-1 equals three
                and x-sub-2 equals two.
            </description>
<image width="50%">
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.55]

% Panel 1
\begin{scope}
\draw[->] (-10,0) -- (10,0) node[right] {$x_1$};
\draw[->] (0,-5) -- (0,5) node[above] {$x_2$};

\draw[line width=1.2pt]
(-10,-2.5) -- (10,4.2);

\draw[line width=1.2pt]
(-10,-4.8) -- (10,5.2);
\end{scope}

% Panel 2
\begin{scope}[xshift=14cm]
\draw[->] (-10,0) -- (10,0) node[right] {$x_1$};
\draw[->] (0,-5) -- (0,5) node[above] {$x_2$};

\draw[line width=1.2pt]
(-10,-4.8) -- (10,5.2);

\draw[line width=1.2pt]
(-10,2) -- (10,2);
\end{scope}

% Panel 3
\begin{scope}[xshift=28cm]
\draw[->] (-10,0) -- (10,0) node[right] {$x_1$};
\draw[->] (0,-5) -- (0,5) node[above] {$x_2$};

\draw[line width=1.2pt]
(3,-5) -- (3,5);

\draw[line width=1.2pt]
(-10,2) -- (10,2);

\fill (3,2) circle (2pt);
\end{scope}

\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
    </statement>
</example>

</handout>
